</span></span></span></span></div>

<div id="abstract1" class="ltx_abstract"><h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_abstract">Abstract</h6>
    
<p id="abstract1.1" class="ltx_p">Multi-LLM collaboration promises
accurate, robust, and context-aware
solutions,
yet existing approaches rely on implicit
selection and output assessment without analyzing whether collaborating models truly
complement or conflict.
We introduce <em id="abstract1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">LLM Chemistry</em> –
a framework
that measures
when LLM combinations exhibit synergistic or
antagonistic behaviors
that shape collective performance beyond individual
capabilities.
We formalize the notion of chemistry among LLMs, propose
algorithms that quantify it by analyzing interaction dependencies,
and recommend optimal model ensembles accordingly.
Our theoretical analysis shows
that chemistry among collaborating LLMs is most evident under heterogeneous model profiles, with its outcome impact shaped by task type, group size, and complexity.
Evaluation on classification, summarization, and program repair tasks provides initial evidence
for these task-dependent effects,
thereby reinforcing our theoretical results.
This establishes LLM Chemistry
as both a diagnostic factor in
multi-LLM systems and
a foundation for ensemble recommendation.</p>
  
</div>
<div id="p1" class="ltx_para">
<table id="p1.1" class="ltx_tabular ltx_align_top">
<tbody class="ltx_tbody">
<tr id="p1.1.1" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="p1.1.1.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_rule" style="width:0.0pt;height:24.0pt;--ltx-bg-color:black;display:inline-block;"></span></td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
</div>
<section id="S1" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">1 </span>Introduction</h2>

<div id="S1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Large Language Models (LLMs) are increasingly capable across tasks from code
generation to open-domain question answering. Yet no single model excels
universally <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chang et al., 2024</a>)</cite>. Different LLMs bring varied
strengths—reasoning, generation, or domain expertise—spurring interest in
multi-LLM collaboration: systems that coordinate multiple models to solve tasks
collectively. By combining complementary abilities, such collaboration promises
more accurate, robust, and context-aware solutions than any single model
alone <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">Feng et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib33" title="" class="ltx_ref">Tran et al., 2025</a>)</cite>, though
a key challenge lies in
selecting ensembles that reliably exploit these strengths.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">To address this challenge,
a growing body of work has proposed numerous collaborative strategies positioned
at different stages of the inference pipeline: (1) before inference,
e.g., LLM routers <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib31" title="" class="ltx_ref">Rosenbaum et al., 2018</a>)</cite>; (2) during inference,
e.g., ensemble decoding <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">Li et al., 2024a</a>; <a href="#bib.bib25" title="" class="ltx_ref">Mavromatis et al., 2024</a>)</cite>; and (3)
after inference,
e.g., LLM Cascades <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chen et al., 2024b</a>; <a href="#bib.bib36" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yue et al., 2024</a>)</cite> and LLM compensatory
cooperation <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib38" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhao et al., 2024</a>)</cite>. These strategies aim to harness the
strengths of multiple and frequently collaborating LLMs.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Existing methods focus primarily on selecting <em id="S1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">strong individual
models</em>—often large, high-performing closed-source LLMs such as ChatGPT,
Claude, and Gemini—
without considering
how their interactions affect group
performance <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hu et al., 2024a</a>)</cite>.
Yet evidence shows
these interactions can substantially influence
collaborative outcomes <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">Liu et al., 2024</a>)</cite> and even transmit unrelated
behavioral traits
via
generated data <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">Cloud et al., 2025</a>)</cite>. Work on
compound AI systems has examined LLM assignment across modules under resource
constraints, but
assumes modular independence and ignores potential
synergy—or interference—when models collaborate on a single task.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">To illustrate how such dynamics play out in practice, consider a <span id="S1.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">statement credibility classification</span> task:
“<span id="S1.p4.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Small businesses create 70 percent of the jobs in America.</span> (Eric
Cantor).” A single model may be precise but brittle (e.g.,
misclassifying unusual variations of this claim as <span id="S1.p4.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">false</span>), while
another may be broader but noisier (e.g., giving inconsistent answers such as
<span id="S1.p4.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">true, mostly true, half true</span>). Individually, neither suffices. Yet when
models are combined and allowed to <span id="S1.p4.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">interact through multiple rounds of
response generation and critique</span>, they may converge on a more accurate
answer—or entrench errors—showing that while multi-model solutions can
outperform individual models, their collective performance depends as much on
interaction as on individual accuracy <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">Du et al., 2023</a>)</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.p5.1" class="ltx_p">Motivated by this, we shift focus from allocation to interaction:
rather than treating LLMs as isolated units, we study how they perform jointly
on a single task, aiming to identify subsets that exhibit strong collaborative
synergy—what we call <span id="S1.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">LLM Chemistry</span>. This raises a central question:
<em id="S1.p5.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_bold ltx_font_italic">For a given task, how can we identify which LLMs work best together, i.e., exhibit strong chemistry?</em></p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p6" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.p6.1" class="ltx_p">To address this question, we
propose a framework for quantifying LLM Chemistry among LLMs collaborating on shared tasks.
We formalize this notion and present algorithms that identify interaction dependencies among models and recommend those with strong chemistry.
Informally, an LLM <math id="S1.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>a</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a</annotation></semantics></math> interacts with an LLM <math id="S1.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>b</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b</annotation></semantics></math> if the benefit of using <math id="S1.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>a</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a</annotation></semantics></math> on
a task changes in the presence of <math id="S1.p6.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>b</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b</annotation></semantics></math>, and vice versa. We evaluate such chemistry purely through <span id="S1.p6.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">performance evaluation</span>, i.e., how effectively
LLMs achieve objectives together.
Our experiments across three benchmarks provide initial evidence that interaction dynamics shape collective performance—sometimes amplifying, sometimes constraining. This suggests model selection alone may be insufficient, since outcomes also depend on interaction effects as well as individual strength.
We model collaboration as a two-stage process of response generation and evaluation,
a common setup in
multi-LLM collaboration frameworks <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">Du et al., 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">Madaan et al., 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib37" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhang et al., 2024</a>)</cite>. In all benchmarks, LLMs interact through this process rather than via independent subgoal assignment.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p7" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.p7.1" class="ltx_p">We summarize our technical contributions as follows:</p>
<ul id="S1.I1" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S1.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We introduce and formalize the notion of LLM Chemistry and present
<span id="S1.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">ChemE</span>, an algorithm for computing
the LLM chemistry within a group of LLMs.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We introduce the concept of Model Interaction Graphs (MIGs) as a mechanism to encode the performance and cost tradeoffs of different LLM interactions in <span id="S1.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">ChemE</span>.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We present <span id="S1.I1.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Recommend</span>, an algorithm for selecting optimal LLM
combinations that exhibit strong chemistry for collaborative tasks.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i4" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We conduct empirical experiments across three diverse benchmarks
demonstrating that interactions significantly influence collaborative
performance and thus LLM Chemistry.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">2 </span>LLM Chemistry</h2>

<section id="S2.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.1 </span>Preliminaries</h3>

<div id="S2.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SS1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Basic Concepts</span>. Let <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q</annotation></semantics></math> be a query that needs answering. Suppose we
have a group of candidate LLMs (denoted by <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math>), each with recorded past
performance (e.g., accuracy of LLMs and a quality score for their outputs). Our
goals are: (1) to quantify how LLMs interact—their <span id="S2.SS1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">chemistry</span>—when
jointly answering <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q</annotation></semantics></math>; (2) to assess the impact of their interactions on
performance; and (3) to identify the optimal <span id="S2.SS1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">configuration</span> (i.e., subset of
LLMs) for <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q</annotation></semantics></math>. Figure <a href="#S4.F2" title="Figure 2 ‣ 4.2 Multi-LLM Recommendation ‣ 4 Computing LLM Chemistry for Multi-LLM Recommendation ‣ LLM Chemistry Estimation for Multi-LLM Recommendation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a> illustrates this process.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Given a configuration <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X\subseteq S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>⊆</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X\subseteq S</annotation></semantics></math> chosen to answer <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q</annotation></semantics></math>, we introduce
<math id="S2.SS1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathit{cost}_{Q}(X)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝑐𝑜𝑠𝑡</mi><mi>Q</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathit{cost}_{Q}(X)</annotation></semantics></math> as the total cost incurred by using <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math>, and
<math id="S2.SS1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathit{used}(X)\subseteq X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>𝑢𝑠𝑒𝑑</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⊆</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathit{used}(X)\subseteq X</annotation></semantics></math> as the subset of LLMs that successfully generated
an answer for <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q</annotation></semantics></math>. Note that <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X\setminus\mathit{used}(X)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>∖</mo><mrow><mi>𝑢𝑠𝑒𝑑</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X\setminus\mathit{used}(X)</annotation></semantics></math> may be non-empty if
some LLMs in <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> did not contribute an output. The cost is defined as follows: assume <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math>
produces <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math> outputs for <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q</annotation></semantics></math>. Each output <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="o_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>o</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">o_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> has a quality score <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q_{i}\in[0,10]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>10</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q_{i}\in[0,10]</annotation></semantics></math> (the consensus grade), each producing LLM has accuracy <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a_{i}\in[0,1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a_{i}\in[0,1]</annotation></semantics></math>
(its reliability score; see below for how <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> are computed), and a
weight <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w_{i}=1/i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w_{i}=1/i</annotation></semantics></math>.<span id="footnote1" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">1</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">1</sup>
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">1</span>
              
              
              
            Weights are positive reals, inversely proportional
to rank, so higher-ranked answers add more to the cost.</span></span></span> Since <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> is in
<math id="S2.SS1.p2.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[0,10]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>10</mn><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[0,10]</annotation></semantics></math>, we normalize it to <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[0,1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[0,1]</annotation></semantics></math> to match accuracy <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>:
<math id="S2.SS1.p2.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q_{i}^{\mathit{norm}}=q_{i}/10" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>q</mi><mi>i</mi><mi>𝑛𝑜𝑟𝑚</mi></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>/</mo><mn>10</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q_{i}^{\mathit{norm}}=q_{i}/10</annotation></semantics></math>.
Accordingly, we define the per-answer <span id="S2.SS1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">penalty</span> (a measure of the joint error in quality and accuracy, which decreases as either improves) as <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathit{penalty}_{i}=(1-q_{i}^{\mathit{norm}})(1-a_{i})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝑝𝑒𝑛𝑎𝑙𝑡𝑦</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msubsup><mi>q</mi><mi>i</mi><mi>𝑛𝑜𝑟𝑚</mi></msubsup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathit{penalty}_{i}=(1-q_{i}^{\mathit{norm}})(1-a_{i})</annotation></semantics></math>.
The total cost over all <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m24" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> answers,
weighted by <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m25" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>, is:</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<table id="S2.E1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathit{cost}_{Q}(X)=\sum_{i=1}^{n}w_{i}\cdot\mathit{penalty}_{i}\," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝑐𝑜𝑠𝑡</mi><mi>Q</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>n</mi></munderover><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msub><mi>𝑝𝑒𝑛𝑎𝑙𝑡𝑦</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathit{cost}_{Q}(X)=\sum_{i=1}^{n}w_{i}\cdot\mathit{penalty}_{i}\,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(1)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">The best-case scenario (<math id="S2.SS1.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q_{i}^{\mathit{norm}}=1,a_{i}=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>q</mi><mi>i</mi><mi>𝑛𝑜𝑟𝑚</mi></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q_{i}^{\mathit{norm}}=1,a_{i}=1</annotation></semantics></math>): penalty is <math id="S2.SS1.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0</annotation></semantics></math>. Lower values in
either quality or accuracy, increase the penalty (thus, the total cost). E.g.,
consider three answers with (rank, quality, accuracy): (1, 9.0, 0.90), (2, 8.0,
0.80), (3, 7.0, 0.70). Using weights <math id="S2.SS1.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w_{i}=1/i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w_{i}=1/i</annotation></semantics></math>,
the total cost is <math id="S2.SS1.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.01+0.02+0.0297=0.0597" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mn>0.01</mn><mo>+</mo><mn>0.02</mn><mo>+</mo><mn>0.0297</mn></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0.0597</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.01+0.02+0.0297=0.0597</annotation></semantics></math>.
This shows low penalties for high-quality, high-accuracy answers, with penalties rising down the ranks; this cost analysis sets the stage for defining LLM benefit within an ensemble.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS1.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SS1.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Presence Benefit of an LLM in an Ensemble</span>. Given Equation <a href="#S2.E1" title="In 2.1 Preliminaries ‣ 2 LLM Chemistry ‣ LLM Chemistry Estimation for Multi-LLM Recommendation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>
and disjoint sets <math id="S2.SS1.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X,Y\subseteq S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow><mo>⊆</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X,Y\subseteq S</annotation></semantics></math>, the <span id="S2.SS1.p5.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">presence benefit</span> of an LLM is
the change in performance cost when <math id="S2.SS1.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y</annotation></semantics></math> is selected in addition to <math id="S2.SS1.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math>,</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmdefinition1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_definition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmdefinition1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 1</span></span><span id="Thmdefinition1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Benefit)<span id="Thmdefinition1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmdefinition1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmdefinition1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmdefinition1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Given two LLM sets <math id="Thmdefinition1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="Thmdefinition1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y\subseteq S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo>⊆</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y\subseteq S</annotation></semantics></math> and a query <math id="Thmdefinition1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q</annotation></semantics></math>, the benefit of <math id="Thmdefinition1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math>
with respect to <math id="Thmdefinition1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="Thmdefinition1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q</annotation></semantics></math> is defined as <math id="Thmdefinition1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathit{benefit}_{Q}(X,\,Y)=\mathit{cost}_{Q}(Y)-\mathit{cost}_{Q}(X\;\cup\;Y)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝑏𝑒𝑛𝑒𝑓𝑖𝑡</mi><mi>Q</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝑐𝑜𝑠𝑡</mi><mi>Q</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝑐𝑜𝑠𝑡</mi><mi>Q</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo lspace="0.502em" rspace="0.502em">∪</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathit{benefit}_{Q}(X,\,Y)=\mathit{cost}_{Q}(Y)-\mathit{cost}_{Q}(X\;\cup\;Y)</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.p6" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS1.p6.1" class="ltx_p">The value of <math id="S2.SS1.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathit{benefit}_{Q}(X,Y)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝑏𝑒𝑛𝑒𝑓𝑖𝑡</mi><mi>Q</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathit{benefit}_{Q}(X,Y)</annotation></semantics></math> can be negative if adding <math id="S2.SS1.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="S2.SS1.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y</annotation></semantics></math>
raises the total cost, indicating degraded rather than improved performance.
Understanding such degradations—and, more broadly, how LLMs affect each
other’s effectiveness—is key to analyzing model chemistry.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.p7" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS1.p7.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SS1.p7.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Quality and Accuracy Scores</span>. We define the quality of an output <math id="S2.SS1.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="o_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>o</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">o_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> (its consensus score <math id="S2.SS1.p7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>) as the
aggregated estimate of its quality from multiple LLMs, and the accuracy of an
LLM (<math id="S2.SS1.p7.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>) as how closely its evaluations align with these consensus grades. We compute quality scores <math id="S2.SS1.p7.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> using a
consensus-based approach inspired by the <span id="S2.SS1.p7.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Vancouver crowdsourcing
algorithm</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">de Alfaro &amp; Shavlovsky, 2014</a>)</cite>. We apply the <span id="S2.SS1.p7.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Minimum Variance
Linear Estimator</span> (MVLE) to iteratively infer consensus scores for each output
and a consensus variance for each LLM. The consensus score becomes <math id="S2.SS1.p7.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>, with
higher values assigned to models whose outputs consistently align with
low-variance consensus. In this setup, as in MVLE, the <span id="S2.SS1.p7.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">inverse variance</span> serves as a
proxy for <span id="S2.SS1.p7.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">review accuracy</span>.
<span id="S2.SS1.p7.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Generation accuracy</span> measures how well an LLM’s outputs match ground truth reference answers, computed by direct comparison when available (values in <math id="S2.SS1.p7.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[0.0,1.0]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mn>0.0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1.0</mn><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[0.0,1.0]</annotation></semantics></math>), or set to <math id="S2.SS1.p7.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0.0</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.0</annotation></semantics></math> when none exist.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.p8" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS1.p8.1" class="ltx_p">To complement this, we compute a <span id="S2.SS1.p8.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">continuous</span> accuracy <math id="S2.SS1.p8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> for an LLM
by combining generation and review accuracy. With ground truth, we weight them
<math id="S2.SS1.p8.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="75\%/25\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mn>75</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><mo>/</mo><mrow><mn>25</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">75\%/25\%</annotation></semantics></math>; without it, <math id="S2.SS1.p8.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="25\%/75\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mn>25</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><mo>/</mo><mrow><mn>75</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">25\%/75\%</annotation></semantics></math>. For example, o3-mini on statement
classification has generation accuracy <math id="S2.SS1.p8.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1.0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1.0</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1.0</annotation></semantics></math> and review accuracy <math id="S2.SS1.p8.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.846" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0.846</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.846</annotation></semantics></math>,
yielding <math id="S2.SS1.p8.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a_{i}=0.962" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0.962</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a_{i}=0.962</annotation></semantics></math>.
We use MVLE over traditional voting to compute <math id="S2.SS1.p8.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS1.p8.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> for its statistical
grounding, robustness to noise, and ability to refine reliability estimates.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.2 </span>The LLM Chemistry Problem</h3>

<div id="S2.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Beyond assessing individual contributions, we aim to understand how LLMs in a
set <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math> interact when jointly answering a query <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q</annotation></semantics></math>. These
interactions—whether cooperative or redundant—can significantly impact the
overall effectiveness of the ensemble. Intuitively, two LLMs <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>a</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>b</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b</annotation></semantics></math>
interact when their individual benefits are dependent. This can occur in two
ways. First, if <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>a</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>b</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b</annotation></semantics></math> exhibit overlapping performance (<math id="S2.SS2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>) profiles—i.e., they produce
similar or redundant outputs on <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q</annotation></semantics></math>—then one can substitute for the other.
Building on our notion of LLM chemistry, this constitutes a <em id="S2.SS2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">negative
chemistry</em>, as using both LLMs adds little benefit over using just one. Second,
if <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>a</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>b</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b</annotation></semantics></math> contribute complementary outputs—e.g., one extracts factual
content while the other interprets it—their combination can yield a better
answer than either alone. This constitutes a <em id="S2.SS2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">positive chemistry</em>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">We formalize this intuition by defining the <span id="S2.SS2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">LLM Chemistry</span> between two
LLMs <math id="S2.SS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>a</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>b</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b</annotation></semantics></math>, denoted <math id="S2.SS2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathit{chem}_{Q}(a,b,S)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝑐ℎ𝑒𝑚</mi><mi>Q</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>S</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathit{chem}_{Q}(a,b,S)</annotation></semantics></math>, as the change in the benefit
of <math id="S2.SS2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>a</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a</annotation></semantics></math> when <math id="S2.SS2.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>b</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b</annotation></semantics></math> is added to a set <math id="S2.SS2.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math>:</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmdefinition2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_definition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmdefinition2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 2</span></span><span id="Thmdefinition2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(LLM Chemistry)<span id="Thmdefinition2.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmdefinition2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="Thmdefinition2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmdefinition2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Given LLMs <math id="Thmdefinition2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>a</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="Thmdefinition2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>b</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="Thmdefinition2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="Thmdefinition2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X\subseteq S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>⊆</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X\subseteq S</annotation></semantics></math> be a set of LLMs in <math id="Thmdefinition2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math> such
that <math id="Thmdefinition2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X\cap\{a,\!b\}=\emptyset" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>∩</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mi>a</mi><mpadded style="width:0.275em;" width="0.275em"><mo>,</mo></mpadded><mi>b</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∅</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X\cap\{a,\!b\}=\emptyset</annotation></semantics></math>, the chemistry between LLMs <math id="Thmdefinition2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>a</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="Thmdefinition2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>b</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b</annotation></semantics></math>
w.r.t. <math id="Thmdefinition2.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q</annotation></semantics></math> is defined as:</span></p>
</div>
<div id="Thmdefinition2.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<table id="S2.E2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathit{chem}_{Q}(a,b,S)=\max_{X\subseteq S\setminus\{a,\!b\}}\frac{\Delta(a,b,X)}{\mathit{cost}_{Q}(X\cup\{a,\!b\})}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝑐ℎ𝑒𝑚</mi><mi>Q</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>S</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><munder><mi>max</mi><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>⊆</mo><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>∖</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo rspace="0.047em">,</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></munder><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝑐𝑜𝑠𝑡</mi><mi>Q</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>∪</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mi>a</mi><mpadded style="width:0.275em;" width="0.275em"><mo>,</mo></mpadded><mi>b</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathit{chem}_{Q}(a,b,S)=\max_{X\subseteq S\setminus\{a,\!b\}}\frac{\Delta(a,b,X)}{\mathit{cost}_{Q}(X\cup\{a,\!b\})}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(2)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="Thmdefinition2.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="Thmdefinition2.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmdefinition2.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">where </span><math id="Thmdefinition2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta(a,b,X)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta(a,b,X)</annotation></semantics></math><span id="Thmdefinition2.p3.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> is the absolute difference between
</span><math id="Thmdefinition2.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathit{benefit}_{Q}(\{a\},X)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝑏𝑒𝑛𝑒𝑓𝑖𝑡</mi><mi>Q</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathit{benefit}_{Q}(\{a\},X)</annotation></semantics></math><span id="Thmdefinition2.p3.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> and </span><math id="Thmdefinition2.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathit{benefit}_{Q}(\{a\},X\cup\{b\})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝑏𝑒𝑛𝑒𝑓𝑖𝑡</mi><mi>Q</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>∪</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathit{benefit}_{Q}(\{a\},X\cup\{b\})</annotation></semantics></math><span id="Thmdefinition2.p3.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">This definition reflects how strongly LLMs <math id="S2.SS2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>a</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS2.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>b</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b</annotation></semantics></math> complement each other
when answering a query <math id="S2.SS2.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q</annotation></semantics></math>. Specifically, the LLM chemistry is highest when <math id="S2.SS2.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>a</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a</annotation></semantics></math>
and <math id="S2.SS2.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>b</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b</annotation></semantics></math> provide highly complementary information, and lowest when their outputs
are redundant, conflicting, or erroneous. To ensure that <math id="S2.SS2.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathit{chem}_{Q}(a,\!b,S)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝑐ℎ𝑒𝑚</mi><mi>Q</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>S</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathit{chem}_{Q}(a,\!b,S)</annotation></semantics></math>
is independent from any specific choice of <math id="S2.SS2.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math>, we define it explicitly as the
maximum interaction effect across all possible subsets <math id="S2.SS2.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X\subseteq S\setminus\{a,\!b\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>⊆</mo><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>∖</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mi>a</mi><mpadded style="width:0.275em;" width="0.275em"><mo>,</mo></mpadded><mi>b</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X\subseteq S\setminus\{a,\!b\}</annotation></semantics></math>. This approach captures the worst-case interaction scenario,
ensuring the measure reflects the strongest potential chemistry between
two LLMs, independent of particular subset selections. For clarity, we omit <math id="S2.SS2.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="S2.SS2.p3.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathit{cost}(X)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝑐𝑜𝑠𝑡</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathit{cost}(X)</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S2.SS2.p3.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathit{benefit}(X,Y)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝑏𝑒𝑛𝑒𝑓𝑖𝑡</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathit{benefit}(X,Y)</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S2.SS2.p3.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathit{chem}(a,b,S)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝑐ℎ𝑒𝑚</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>S</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathit{chem}(a,b,S)</annotation></semantics></math> when it is clear from context.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS2.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS2.p4.1" class="ltx_p">The LLM chemistry between any pair of LLMs is critical to understanding how the
set <math id="S2.SS2.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math> influences answers to <math id="S2.SS2.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q</annotation></semantics></math>. This leads to a key problem addressed in this
paper:</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmproblem1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_problem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmproblem1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Problem 1</span></span><span id="Thmproblem1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(LLMs Chemistry Problem (<span id="Thmproblem1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">LLMCP</span>))<span id="Thmproblem1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmproblem1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmproblem1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmproblem1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Given a set of LLMs <math id="Thmproblem1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math>, a subset <math id="Thmproblem1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X\subseteq S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>⊆</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X\subseteq S</annotation></semantics></math>, and a threshold <math id="Thmproblem1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau\geq 0.0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>0.0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau\geq 0.0</annotation></semantics></math>, find all pairs of distinct LLMs <math id="Thmproblem1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a,\!b\in S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>a</mi><mpadded style="width:0.275em;" width="0.275em"><mo>,</mo></mpadded><mi>b</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a,\!b\in S</annotation></semantics></math> such that
<math id="Thmproblem1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathit{chem}(a,b,S)&gt;\tau" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>𝑐ℎ𝑒𝑚</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>S</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mi>τ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathit{chem}(a,b,S)&gt;\tau</annotation></semantics></math> w.r.t. <math id="Thmproblem1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS2.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS2.p5.1" class="ltx_p">In this problem, we aim to identify pairs of LLMs that exhibit sufficiently
strong chemistry for answering <math id="S2.SS2.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q</annotation></semantics></math>. The goal is not to compute exact chemistry
values, but to decide whether chemistry is sufficiently high. Detecting strong
chemistry requires finding only a single LLM configuration where threshold
<math id="S2.SS2.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>τ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau</annotation></semantics></math> is exceeded, while ruling it out requires checking all relevant
configurations. Thus, worst-case complexity remains as high as computing full
LLM Chemistry.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S3" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">3 </span>Criteria for Optimal LLM Selection</h2>

<div id="S3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In our formulation of <span id="S3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">LLMCP</span>, LLM interactions are evaluated by <math id="S3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathit{cost}(X)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝑐𝑜𝑠𝑡</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathit{cost}(X)</annotation></semantics></math> (Equation <a href="#S2.E1" title="In 2.1 Preliminaries ‣ 2 LLM Chemistry ‣ LLM Chemistry Estimation for Multi-LLM Recommendation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>) to determine the optimal LLM selection for a query <math id="S3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q</annotation></semantics></math>. This requires computing <math id="S3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathit{chem}(a,b,S)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝑐ℎ𝑒𝑚</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>S</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathit{chem}(a,b,S)</annotation></semantics></math> for every LLM pair <math id="S3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a,b" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a,b</annotation></semantics></math> across all subsets <math id="S3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X\subseteq S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>⊆</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X\subseteq S</annotation></semantics></math>, giving a lower bound of <math id="S3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Omega(2^{|S|},|S|^{2})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>S</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>S</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Omega(2^{|S|},|S|^{2})</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">In practice, the optimization of LLM selection should not depend on a particular
set <math id="S3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> in an arbitrary way. For instance, adding more LLMs to the available set
should not increase the cost of answering <math id="S3.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q</annotation></semantics></math>, since a larger set only broadens
the space of possible responses. To avoid such anomalies, we make a natural
assumption on <math id="S3.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math>. Conceptually, we assume <math id="S3.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math> is made of diverse and
independently strong LLMs whose strengths compensate for one another’s
weaknesses, rather than reinforce them. Informally, we say <math id="S3.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math> is
<span id="S3.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">diverse</span> if its members exhibit heterogeneous performance
characteristics—i.e., they differ meaningfully in their performance (<math id="S3.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S3.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>)
profiles. Conversely, <math id="S3.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math> is <span id="S3.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">homogeneous</span> if all models produce similar
responses with comparable accuracy (i.e., aligned performance profiles).</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.p3.1" class="ltx_p">This diversity assumption has profound implications for cost function
behavior and thus on LLM Chemistry estimation. Under this assumption,
<math id="S3.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathit{cost}(X)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝑐𝑜𝑠𝑡</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathit{cost}(X)</annotation></semantics></math> should exhibit structure that reflects the reliability and
diversity of the underlying models. Rather than treating all LLM combinations as equally unpredictable, we posit that cost varies predictably with model quality, accuracy, and set composition. This motivates
three key properties of the cost function. These properties—monotonicity,
linearity, and submodularity—not only align with practical expectations but
also play a central role in making the selection of optimal LLM sets
computationally feasible.</p>
</div>
<section id="S3.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.1 </span>Properties of the Cost Function</h3>

<div id="S3.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We formalize three properties that characterize Equation <a href="#S2.E1" title="In 2.1 Preliminaries ‣ 2 LLM Chemistry ‣ LLM Chemistry Estimation for Multi-LLM Recommendation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>:</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmproperty1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_property">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmproperty1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Property 1</span></span><span id="Thmproperty1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Monotonicity)<span id="Thmproperty1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmproperty1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="Thmproperty1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmproperty1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">The cost of a set <math id="Thmproperty1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> for answering <math id="Thmproperty1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="Thmproperty1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathit{cost}(X)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝑐𝑜𝑠𝑡</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathit{cost}(X)</annotation></semantics></math>, is
monotonically decreasing with the output quality scores <math id="Thmproperty1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> and the accuracy values <math id="Thmproperty1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> of the LLMs producing them.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">This property means the cost decreases as an LLM’s accuracy or output
quality increases, reflecting the intuition that accurate, high-quality outputs
lower the total cost. (Proof in Appendix <a href="#A1.SS1" title="A.1 Proofs of Cost Function Properties ‣ Appendix A Appendix ‣ LLM Chemistry Estimation for Multi-LLM Recommendation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A.1</span></a>.)</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmproperty2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_property">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmproperty2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Property 2</span></span><span id="Thmproperty2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Linearity of Cost)<span id="Thmproperty2.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmproperty2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmproperty2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmproperty2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">The <math id="Thmproperty2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathit{cost}(X)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝑐𝑜𝑠𝑡</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathit{cost}(X)</annotation></semantics></math> separates as a sum of terms, each depending
only on the quality <math id="Thmproperty2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> of an individual LLM output and the accuracy <math id="Thmproperty2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> of its producing LLM.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">This property means the cost is additive: each LLM affects the total cost only
through its own contribution, with no cross-terms or interactions between
outputs. (Proof in Appendix <a href="#A1.SS1" title="A.1 Proofs of Cost Function Properties ‣ Appendix A Appendix ‣ LLM Chemistry Estimation for Multi-LLM Recommendation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A.1</span></a>.)</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmproperty3" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_property">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmproperty3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Property 3</span></span><span id="Thmproperty3.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Submodularity)<span id="Thmproperty3.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmproperty3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmproperty3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmproperty3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">For all sets <math id="Thmproperty3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X\subseteq Y\subseteq S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>⊆</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo>⊆</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X\subseteq Y\subseteq S</annotation></semantics></math> and any LLM <math id="Thmproperty3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a\in S\setminus Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>∖</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a\in S\setminus Y</annotation></semantics></math>,
Equation <a href="#S2.E1" title="In 2.1 Preliminaries ‣ 2 LLM Chemistry ‣ LLM Chemistry Estimation for Multi-LLM Recommendation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> satisfies: <math id="Thmproperty3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathit{cost}(X)-\mathit{cost}(X\cup\{a\})\geq\mathit{cost}(Y)-\mathit{cost}(Y\cup\{a\})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>𝑐𝑜𝑠𝑡</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>𝑐𝑜𝑠𝑡</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>∪</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>𝑐𝑜𝑠𝑡</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>𝑐𝑜𝑠𝑡</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo>∪</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathit{cost}(X)-\mathit{cost}(X\cup\{a\})\geq\mathit{cost}(Y)-\mathit{cost}(Y\cup\{a\})</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">This property implies diminishing returns: for any given LLM <math id="S3.SS1.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>a</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a</annotation></semantics></math>, its marginal benefit is larger when added to a smaller set <math id="S3.SS1.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> than to a larger superset <math id="S3.SS1.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y</annotation></semantics></math>. (Proof in Appendix <a href="#A1.SS1" title="A.1 Proofs of Cost Function Properties ‣ Appendix A Appendix ‣ LLM Chemistry Estimation for Multi-LLM Recommendation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A.1</span></a>.)</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.2 </span>Model Interaction Graphs (MIGs)</h3>

<div id="S3.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To efficiently compute LLM chemistry, we introduce the <span id="S3.SS2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Model Interaction
Graph</span> (MIG), a directed acyclic graph (DAG) over subsets of <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math>. The MIG
encoding draws inspiration from the <span id="S3.SS2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Index Benefit Graph</span> (IBG)
<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">Frank et al., 1992</a>)</cite>. Figure <a href="#S3.F1" title="Figure 1 ‣ 3.2 Model Interaction Graphs (MIGs) ‣ 3 Criteria for Optimal LLM Selection ‣ LLM Chemistry Estimation for Multi-LLM Recommendation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> shows an MIG for <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S\!=\!\{a,b,c\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo lspace="0.108em" rspace="0.108em">=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S\!=\!\{a,b,c\}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S3.F1" class="ltx_figure ltx_align_floatright" style="width:49%;"><span class="ltx_inline-block"><svg id="S3.F1.pic1" class="ltx_picture ltx_centering" height="133.62" overflow="visible" version="1.1" viewBox="0 0 215.47 133.62" width="215.47"><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" transform="translate(0,133.62) matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 0) translate(105.92,0) translate(0,125.38)"><g stroke-width="0.4pt"><g style="--ltx-fill-color:#F2F2F2;" fill="#F2F2F2"><path d="M 33.55 7.96 L -33.55 7.96 C -36.61 7.96 -39.09 5.48 -39.09 2.42 L -39.09 -2.42 C -39.09 -5.48 -36.61 -7.96 -33.55 -7.96 L 33.55 -7.96 C 36.61 -7.96 39.09 -5.48 39.09 -2.42 L 39.09 2.42 C 39.09 5.48 36.61 7.96 33.55 7.96 Z M -39.09 -7.96"></path></g><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 -36.32 -3.27)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:4.5em;--ltx-fo-height:0.58em;--ltx-fo-depth:0.13em;font-size:10.5pt;" height="10.38" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 8.46)" width="65.38"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><span id="S3.F1.pic1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_underline ltx_font_typewriter">a</span><span id="S3.F1.pic1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">,b,<span id="S3.F1.pic1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_underline">c</span>:0.05</span></span></span></foreignObject></g><g style="--ltx-fill-color:#F2F2F2;" fill="#F2F2F2"><path d="M -47.53 -51.1 L -100.11 -51.1 C -103.17 -51.1 -105.64 -53.58 -105.64 -56.63 L -105.64 -61.48 C -105.64 -64.53 -103.17 -67.01 -100.11 -67.01 L -47.53 -67.01 C -44.47 -67.01 -41.99 -64.53 -41.99 -61.48 L -41.99 -56.63 C -41.99 -53.58 -44.47 -51.1 -47.53 -51.1 Z M -105.64 -67.01"></path></g><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 -102.88 -62.32)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:4em;--ltx-fo-height:0.58em;--ltx-fo-depth:0.13em;font-size:10.5pt;" height="10.38" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 8.46)" width="58.12"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><span id="S3.F1.pic1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_underline ltx_font_typewriter">a</span><span id="S3.F1.pic1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">,b:0.08</span></span></span></foreignObject></g><g style="--ltx-fill-color:#F2F2F2;" fill="#F2F2F2"><path d="M 26.29 -51.1 L -26.29 -51.1 C -29.35 -51.1 -31.82 -53.58 -31.82 -56.63 L -31.82 -61.48 C -31.82 -64.53 -29.35 -67.01 -26.29 -67.01 L 26.29 -67.01 C 29.35 -67.01 31.82 -64.53 31.82 -61.48 L 31.82 -56.63 C 31.82 -53.58 29.35 -51.1 26.29 -51.1 Z M -31.82 -67.01"></path></g><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 -29.06 -62.32)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:3.5em;--ltx-fo-height:0.58em;--ltx-fo-depth:0.13em;font-size:10.5pt;" height="10.38" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 8.46)" width="50.85"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><span id="S3.F1.pic1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">a,<span id="S3.F1.pic1.5.1" class="ltx_text ltx_underline">c</span>:0.07</span></span></span></foreignObject></g><g style="--ltx-fill-color:#F2F2F2;" fill="#F2F2F2"><path d="M 103.74 -51.1 L 43.9 -51.1 C 40.84 -51.1 38.36 -53.58 38.36 -56.63 L 38.36 -61.48 C 38.36 -64.53 40.84 -67.01 43.9 -67.01 L 103.74 -67.01 C 106.8 -67.01 109.28 -64.53 109.28 -61.48 L 109.28 -56.63 C 109.28 -53.58 106.8 -51.1 103.74 -51.1 Z M 38.36 -67.01"></path></g><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 41.13 -62.32)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:4.5em;--ltx-fo-height:0.58em;--ltx-fo-depth:0.13em;font-size:10.5pt;" height="10.38" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 8.46)" width="65.38"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><span id="S3.F1.pic1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_underline ltx_font_typewriter">b</span><span id="S3.F1.pic1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">,c:0.006</span></span></span></foreignObject></g><g style="--ltx-fill-color:#F2F2F2;" fill="#F2F2F2"><path d="M -51.16 -111.11 L -96.48 -111.11 C -99.53 -111.11 -102.01 -113.59 -102.01 -116.65 L -102.01 -119.57 C -102.01 -122.63 -99.53 -125.11 -96.48 -125.11 L -51.16 -125.11 C -48.1 -125.11 -45.63 -122.63 -45.63 -119.57 L -45.63 -116.65 C -45.63 -113.59 -48.1 -111.11 -51.16 -111.11 Z M -102.01 -125.11"></path></g><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 -99.24 -122.34)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:3.5em;--ltx-fo-height:0.58em;--ltx-fo-depth:0em;font-size:10.5pt;" height="8.46" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 8.46)" width="50.85"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><span id="S3.F1.pic1.8" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">a:0.010</span></span></span></foreignObject></g><g style="--ltx-fill-color:#F2F2F2;" fill="#F2F2F2"><path d="M 22.66 -111.11 L -22.66 -111.11 C -25.71 -111.11 -28.19 -113.59 -28.19 -116.65 L -28.19 -119.57 C -28.19 -122.63 -25.71 -125.11 -22.66 -125.11 L 22.66 -125.11 C 25.71 -125.11 28.19 -122.63 28.19 -119.57 L 28.19 -116.65 C 28.19 -113.59 25.71 -111.11 22.66 -111.11 Z M -28.19 -125.11"></path></g><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 -25.43 -122.34)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:3.5em;--ltx-fo-height:0.58em;--ltx-fo-depth:0em;font-size:10.5pt;" height="8.46" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 8.46)" width="50.85"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><span id="S3.F1.pic1.9" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">b:0.012</span></span></span></foreignObject></g></g><g stroke-width="0.8pt"><path style="fill:none" d="M -10.29 -8.23 L -58.88 -47.1"></path><g stroke-dasharray="none" stroke-dashoffset="0.0pt" stroke-linejoin="miter" transform="matrix(-0.7809 -0.62466 0.62466 -0.7809 -56.4 -45.12)"><path d="M 7.66 0 L 1.79 2.18 L 3.45 0 L 1.79 -2.18 Z"></path></g></g><g stroke-width="0.8pt"><path style="fill:none" d="M 0 -8.23 L 0 -44.86"></path><g stroke-dasharray="none" stroke-dashoffset="0.0pt" stroke-linejoin="miter" transform="matrix(0.0 -1.0 1.0 0.0 0 -41.69)"><path d="M 7.66 0 L 1.79 2.18 L 3.45 0 L 1.79 -2.18 Z"></path></g></g><g stroke-width="0.8pt"><path style="fill:none" d="M 10.29 -8.23 L 58.88 -47.1"></path><g stroke-dasharray="none" stroke-dashoffset="0.0pt" stroke-linejoin="miter" transform="matrix(0.7809 -0.62466 0.62466 0.7809 56.4 -45.12)"><path d="M 7.66 0 L 1.79 2.18 L 3.45 0 L 1.79 -2.18 Z"></path></g></g><g stroke-width="0.8pt"><path style="fill:none" d="M -73.82 -67.29 L -73.82 -104.88"></path><g stroke-dasharray="none" stroke-dashoffset="0.0pt" stroke-linejoin="miter" transform="matrix(0.0 -1.0 1.0 0.0 -73.82 -101.71)"><path d="M 7.66 0 L 1.79 2.18 L 3.45 0 L 1.79 -2.18 Z"></path></g></g><g stroke-width="0.8pt"><path style="fill:none" d="M -63.53 -67.29 L -13.74 -107.11"></path><g stroke-dasharray="none" stroke-dashoffset="0.0pt" stroke-linejoin="miter" transform="matrix(0.78088 -0.62466 0.62466 0.78088 -16.22 -105.13)"><path d="M 7.66 0 L 1.79 2.18 L 3.45 0 L 1.79 -2.18 Z"></path></g></g><g stroke-width="0.8pt"><path style="fill:none" d="M -10.29 -67.29 L -60.08 -107.11"></path><g stroke-dasharray="none" stroke-dashoffset="0.0pt" stroke-linejoin="miter" transform="matrix(-0.78088 -0.62466 0.62466 -0.78088 -57.6 -105.13)"><path d="M 7.66 0 L 1.79 2.18 L 3.45 0 L 1.79 -2.18 Z"></path></g></g><g stroke-width="0.8pt"><path style="fill:none" d="M 63.53 -67.29 L 13.74 -107.11"></path><g stroke-dasharray="none" stroke-dashoffset="0.0pt" stroke-linejoin="miter" transform="matrix(-0.78088 -0.62466 0.62466 -0.78088 16.22 -105.13)"><path d="M 7.66 0 L 1.79 2.18 L 3.45 0 L 1.79 -2.18 Z"></path></g></g></g></svg></span>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 1: </span>MIG for <math id="S3.F1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S\!=\!\{a,b,c\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo lspace="0.108em" rspace="0.108em">=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S\!=\!\{a,b,c\}</annotation></semantics></math>. Underlined elements indicate <math id="S3.F1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathit{used}(X)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝑢𝑠𝑒𝑑</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathit{used}(X)</annotation></semantics></math>
(LLMs with <math id="S3.F1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a_{i}\geq 0.5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>≥</mo><mn>0.5</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a_{i}\geq 0.5</annotation></semantics></math>). Sample cost values are provided in each node.
</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S3.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">A central property of MIGs, inherited from IBGs, is that reasoning about subsets does not
require every subset to be explicitly represented: larger nodes contain enough
information to compute <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathit{cost}(X)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝑐𝑜𝑠𝑡</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathit{cost}(X)</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathit{used}(X)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝑢𝑠𝑒𝑑</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathit{used}(X)</annotation></semantics></math> for overlapping
subsets, allowing the MIG to compactly encode all necessary information and
avoid exponential blowup. Each node in the MIG represents an LLM set <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X\subseteq S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>⊆</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X\subseteq S</annotation></semantics></math> and stores both <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathit{used}(X)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝑢𝑠𝑒𝑑</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathit{used}(X)</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathit{cost}(X)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝑐𝑜𝑠𝑡</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathit{cost}(X)</annotation></semantics></math>. Edges capture how costs change as LLMs are added or
removed. Nodes and edges are constructed hierarchically, starting from the full
set <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math>; at each step, for each node <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> and used LLM <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a\in\mathit{used}(X)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>𝑢𝑠𝑒𝑑</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a\in\mathit{used}(X)</annotation></semantics></math>,
we create a new node <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X^{\prime}=X\setminus{a}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>X</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>∖</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X^{\prime}=X\setminus{a}</annotation></semantics></math> with a directed edge from <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> to
<math id="S3.SS2.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>X</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>, unless <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>X</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> already exists.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S4" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">4 </span>Computing LLM Chemistry for Multi-LLM Recommendation</h2>

<section id="S4.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.1 </span>Computing LLM Chemistry</h3>

<figure id="algorithm1" class="ltx_figure ltx_algorithm ltx_minipage ltx_align_middle ltx_align_floatright ltx_framed ltx_framed_top" style="width:210.7pt;">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="algorithm1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Algorithm 1</span> </span>Computes <math id="algorithm1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathit{chem}(a,b,S)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝑐ℎ𝑒𝑚</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>S</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathit{chem}(a,b,S)</annotation></semantics></math> for all LLM pairs <math id="algorithm1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a,b" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a,b</annotation></semantics></math> using memoized MIG lookups. <math id="algorithm1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{G}_{Q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><mi>Q</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{G}_{Q}</annotation></semantics></math> encodes subset relationships. Solves <span id="algorithm1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">LLMCP</span>. Sets <math id="algorithm1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau=0</annotation></semantics></math> to retain all chemistry information for later analysis.</figcaption>
<div id="algorithm1.6" class="ltx_listing ltx_lst_numbers_left ltx_framed ltx_framed_topbottom ltx_listing">
<div id="algorithm1.6.1" class="ltx_listingline"><span id="algorithm1.6.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Function :</span> <span id="algorithm1.6.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_markedasmath ltx_font_smallcaps">ChemE</span>
</div>
<div id="algorithm1.6.2" class="ltx_listingline"><span id="algorithm1.6.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Input :</span> A set of LLMs <math id="algorithm1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math>.
</div>
<div id="algorithm1.6.3" class="ltx_listingline"><span id="algorithm1.6.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Output :</span> The LLM chemistry for each distinct <math id="algorithm1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a,\!b\in S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>a</mi><mpadded style="width:0.275em;" width="0.275em"><mo>,</mo></mpadded><mi>b</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a,\!b\in S</annotation></semantics></math>.
</div>
<div id="algorithm1.6.4" class="ltx_listingline"><span id="algorithm1.6.4.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Data :</span> Hash table <math id="algorithm1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t_{Q}:S\times S\rightarrow\mathbb{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>Q</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>ℝ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t_{Q}:S\times S\rightarrow\mathbb{R}</annotation></semantics></math>. Memoized node-and-cost lookup.
</div>
<div id="algorithm1.6.5" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="algorithm1.6.5.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">1</span></span>
            Initialize <math id="algorithm1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t_{Q}[a,b]\leftarrow 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>Q</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t_{Q}[a,b]\leftarrow 0</annotation></semantics></math> for each distinct <math id="algorithm1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a,b\subseteq S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><mo>⊆</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a,b\subseteq S</annotation></semantics></math>
</div>
<div id="algorithm1.6.6" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="algorithm1.6.6.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">2</span></span>
            Construct the MIG <math id="algorithm1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{G}_{Q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><mi>Q</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{G}_{Q}</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="algorithm1.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q</annotation></semantics></math>
</div>
<div id="algorithm1.6.7" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="algorithm1.6.7.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">3</span></span>
            <span id="algorithm1.6.7.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">for</span> <em id="algorithm1.6.7.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic"><math id="algorithm1.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k\leftarrow 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k\leftarrow 0</annotation></semantics></math> <span id="algorithm1.6.7.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold ltx_font_upright">to</span> <math id="algorithm1.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|S|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>S</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|S|</annotation></semantics></math></em> <span id="algorithm1.6.7.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">do</span>
</div>
<div id="algorithm1.6.8" class="ltx_listingline"> <span class="ltx_rule" style="width:1px;height:100%;--ltx-bg-color:black;display:inline-block;"> </span> 
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="algorithm1.6.8.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">4</span></span>
            <span id="algorithm1.6.8.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">foreach</span> <em id="algorithm1.6.8.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic"><math id="algorithm1.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X\subseteq S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>⊆</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X\subseteq S</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="algorithm1.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|X|=k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|X|=k</annotation></semantics></math></em> <span id="algorithm1.6.8.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">do</span>
</div>
<div id="algorithm1.6.9" class="ltx_listingline"> <span class="ltx_rule" style="width:1px;height:100%;--ltx-bg-color:black;display:inline-block;"> </span>  <span class="ltx_rule" style="width:1px;height:100%;--ltx-bg-color:black;display:inline-block;"> </span> 
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="algorithm1.6.9.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">5</span></span>
            Let <math id="algorithm1.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y\supseteq X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo>⊇</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y\supseteq X</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="algorithm1.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{G}_{Q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><mi>Q</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{G}_{Q}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="algorithm1.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathit{cost}(Y)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝑐𝑜𝑠𝑡</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathit{cost}(Y)</annotation></semantics></math> be memoized
</div>
<div id="algorithm1.6.10" class="ltx_listingline"> <span class="ltx_rule" style="width:1px;height:100%;--ltx-bg-color:black;display:inline-block;"> </span>  <span class="ltx_rule" style="width:1px;height:100%;--ltx-bg-color:black;display:inline-block;"> </span> 
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="algorithm1.6.10.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">6</span></span>
            <span id="algorithm1.6.10.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">if</span> <em id="algorithm1.6.10.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic"><math id="algorithm1.m24" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y</annotation></semantics></math> is undefined</em> <span id="algorithm1.6.10.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">then</span>
</div>
<div id="algorithm1.6.11" class="ltx_listingline"> <span class="ltx_rule" style="width:1px;height:100%;--ltx-bg-color:black;display:inline-block;"> </span>  <span class="ltx_rule" style="width:1px;height:100%;--ltx-bg-color:black;display:inline-block;"> </span>  <span class="ltx_rule" style="width:1px;height:100%;--ltx-bg-color:black;display:inline-block;"> </span> 
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="algorithm1.6.11.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">7</span></span>
            <span id="algorithm1.6.11.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">continue</span>
</div>
<div id="algorithm1.6.12" class="ltx_listingline"> <span class="ltx_rule" style="width:1px;height:100%;--ltx-bg-color:black;display:inline-block;"> </span>  <span class="ltx_rule" style="width:1px;height:100%;--ltx-bg-color:black;display:inline-block;"> </span> 
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="algorithm1.6.12.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">8</span></span>
            <span id="algorithm1.6.12.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">foreach</span> <em id="algorithm1.6.12.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">distinct <math id="algorithm1.m25" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a,b\in S-X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a,b\in S-X</annotation></semantics></math></em> <span id="algorithm1.6.12.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">do</span>
</div>
<div id="algorithm1.6.13" class="ltx_listingline"> <span class="ltx_rule" style="width:1px;height:100%;--ltx-bg-color:black;display:inline-block;"> </span>  <span class="ltx_rule" style="width:1px;height:100%;--ltx-bg-color:black;display:inline-block;"> </span>  <span class="ltx_rule" style="width:1px;height:100%;--ltx-bg-color:black;display:inline-block;"> </span> 
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="algorithm1.6.13.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">9</span></span>
            Let <math id="algorithm1.m26" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{a}\leftarrow X\cup\{a\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>a</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>∪</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{a}\leftarrow X\cup\{a\}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="algorithm1.m27" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{b}\leftarrow X\cup\{b\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>b</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>∪</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{b}\leftarrow X\cup\{b\}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="algorithm1.m28" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{ab}\leftarrow X\cup\{a,b\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>∪</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{ab}\leftarrow X\cup\{a,b\}</annotation></semantics></math>
</div>
<div id="algorithm1.6.14" class="ltx_listingline"> <span class="ltx_rule" style="width:1px;height:100%;--ltx-bg-color:black;display:inline-block;"> </span>  <span class="ltx_rule" style="width:1px;height:100%;--ltx-bg-color:black;display:inline-block;"> </span>  <span class="ltx_rule" style="width:1px;height:100%;--ltx-bg-color:black;display:inline-block;"> </span> 
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="algorithm1.6.14.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">10</span></span>
            Memoize <math id="algorithm1.m29" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y_{a}\supseteq X_{a}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>a</mi></msub><mo>⊇</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>a</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y_{a}\supseteq X_{a}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="algorithm1.m30" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y_{b}\supseteq X_{b}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>b</mi></msub><mo>⊇</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>b</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y_{b}\supseteq X_{b}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="algorithm1.m31" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y_{ab}\supseteq X_{ab}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Y</mi><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow></msub><mo>⊇</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y_{ab}\supseteq X_{ab}</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="algorithm1.m32" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{G}_{Q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><mi>Q</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{G}_{Q}</annotation></semantics></math> and their costs
</div>
<div id="algorithm1.6.15" class="ltx_listingline"> <span class="ltx_rule" style="width:1px;height:100%;--ltx-bg-color:black;display:inline-block;"> </span>  <span class="ltx_rule" style="width:1px;height:100%;--ltx-bg-color:black;display:inline-block;"> </span>  <span class="ltx_rule" style="width:1px;height:100%;--ltx-bg-color:black;display:inline-block;"> </span> 
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="algorithm1.6.15.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">11</span></span>
            <span id="algorithm1.6.15.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">if</span> <em id="algorithm1.6.15.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">any of <math id="algorithm1.m33" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y_{a}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>a</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y_{a}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="algorithm1.m34" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y_{b}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>b</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y_{b}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="algorithm1.m35" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y_{ab}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>Y</mi><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y_{ab}</annotation></semantics></math> are undefined</em> <span id="algorithm1.6.15.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">then</span>
</div>
<div id="algorithm1.6.16" class="ltx_listingline"> <span class="ltx_rule" style="width:1px;height:100%;--ltx-bg-color:black;display:inline-block;"> </span>  <span class="ltx_rule" style="width:1px;height:100%;--ltx-bg-color:black;display:inline-block;"> </span>  <span class="ltx_rule" style="width:1px;height:100%;--ltx-bg-color:black;display:inline-block;"> </span>  <span class="ltx_rule" style="width:1px;height:100%;--ltx-bg-color:black;display:inline-block;"> </span> 
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="algorithm1.6.16.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">12</span></span>
            <span id="algorithm1.6.16.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">continue</span>
</div>
<div id="algorithm1.6.17" class="ltx_listingline"> <span class="ltx_rule" style="width:1px;height:100%;--ltx-bg-color:black;display:inline-block;"> </span>  <span class="ltx_rule" style="width:1px;height:100%;--ltx-bg-color:black;display:inline-block;"> </span>  <span class="ltx_rule" style="width:1px;height:100%;--ltx-bg-color:black;display:inline-block;"> </span> 
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="algorithm1.6.17.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">13</span></span>
            <math id="algorithm1.m36" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d\leftarrow\frac{|\mathit{benefit}(\{a\},Y)-\mathit{benefit}(\{a\},Y_{b})|}{\mathit{cost}(Y_{ab})}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mfrac><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>𝑏𝑒𝑛𝑒𝑓𝑖𝑡</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>𝑏𝑒𝑛𝑒𝑓𝑖𝑡</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>b</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>𝑐𝑜𝑠𝑡</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d\leftarrow\frac{|\mathit{benefit}(\{a\},Y)-\mathit{benefit}(\{a\},Y_{b})|}{\mathit{cost}(Y_{ab})}</annotation></semantics></math>
</div>
<div id="algorithm1.6.18" class="ltx_listingline"> <span class="ltx_rule" style="width:1px;height:100%;--ltx-bg-color:black;display:inline-block;"> </span>  <span class="ltx_rule" style="width:1px;height:100%;--ltx-bg-color:black;display:inline-block;"> </span>  <span class="ltx_rule" style="width:1px;height:100%;--ltx-bg-color:black;display:inline-block;"> </span> 
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="algorithm1.6.18.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">14</span></span>
            <math id="algorithm1.m37" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t_{Q}[a,b]\leftarrow\max\{t_{Q}[a,b],d\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>Q</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mrow><mi>max</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>Q</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t_{Q}[a,b]\leftarrow\max\{t_{Q}[a,b],d\}</annotation></semantics></math>
</div>
<div id="algorithm1.6.19" class="ltx_listingline"> <span class="ltx_rule" style="width:1px;height:100%;--ltx-bg-color:black;display:inline-block;"> </span>  <span class="ltx_rule" style="width:1px;height:100%;--ltx-bg-color:black;display:inline-block;"> </span>  <span class="ltx_rule" style="width:1px;height:100%;--ltx-bg-color:black;display:inline-block;"> </span> 
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="algorithm1.6.19.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">15</span></span>
            <math id="algorithm1.m38" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t_{Q}[b,a]\leftarrow\max\{t_{Q}[b,a],d\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>Q</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mrow><mi>max</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>Q</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t_{Q}[b,a]\leftarrow\max\{t_{Q}[b,a],d\}</annotation></semantics></math>
</div>
<div id="algorithm1.6.20" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="algorithm1.6.20.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">16</span></span>
            <span id="algorithm1.6.20.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">return</span> <em id="algorithm1.6.20.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic"><math id="algorithm1.m39" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathit{chem}(a,b,S)=t_{Q}[a,b]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>𝑐ℎ𝑒𝑚</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>S</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>Q</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathit{chem}(a,b,S)=t_{Q}[a,b]</annotation></semantics></math> for each <math id="algorithm1.m40" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{a,b\}\subseteq S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo>⊆</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{a,b\}\subseteq S</annotation></semantics></math></em>
</div>
<div id="algorithm1.6.21" class="ltx_listingline">
</div>
</div>
</figure>
<div id="S4.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this section, we introduce <span id="S4.SS1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">ChemE</span> (Algorithm <a href="#algorithm1" title="In 4.1 Computing LLM Chemistry ‣ 4 Computing LLM Chemistry for Multi-LLM Recommendation ‣ LLM Chemistry Estimation for Multi-LLM Recommendation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>).
Given a query <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q</annotation></semantics></math>,
<span id="S4.SS1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">ChemE</span> builds a MIG and looks at its structure
to compute <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathit{chem}(a,b,S)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝑐ℎ𝑒𝑚</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>S</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathit{chem}(a,b,S)</annotation></semantics></math> for every pair of LLMs in <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math> w.r.t. <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q</annotation></semantics></math>.
It does so by iterating over
all subsets <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X\subseteq S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>⊆</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X\subseteq S</annotation></semantics></math> to compactly encode
costs associated with various combinations of LLMs over relevant subsets of <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math>.
<span id="S4.SS1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">ChemE</span> explicitly enumerates subsets to compute this
maximum value, directly aligning with Definition <a href="#Thmdefinition2" title="Definition 2 (LLM Chemistry). ‣ 2.2 The LLM Chemistry Problem ‣ 2 LLM Chemistry ‣ LLM Chemistry Estimation for Multi-LLM Recommendation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">For example, given the MIG in Figure <a href="#S3.F1" title="Figure 1 ‣ 3.2 Model Interaction Graphs (MIGs) ‣ 3 Criteria for Optimal LLM Selection ‣ LLM Chemistry Estimation for Multi-LLM Recommendation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>, assume additional hypothetical costs
<math id="S4.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathit{cost}(\{c\})=0.15" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>𝑐𝑜𝑠𝑡</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0.15</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathit{cost}(\{c\})=0.15</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathit{cost}(\emptyset)=0.20" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>𝑐𝑜𝑠𝑡</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∅</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0.20</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathit{cost}(\emptyset)=0.20</annotation></semantics></math>, and the
case where <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X=\{c\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X=\{c\}</annotation></semantics></math>. Using the MIG costs, we have <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathit{benefit}(\{a\},\{c\})=0.08" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>𝑏𝑒𝑛𝑒𝑓𝑖𝑡</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0.08</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathit{benefit}(\{a\},\{c\})=0.08</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathit{benefit}(\{a\},\{b,c\})=0.01" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>𝑏𝑒𝑛𝑒𝑓𝑖𝑡</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0.01</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathit{benefit}(\{a\},\{b,c\})=0.01</annotation></semantics></math>, yielding
<math id="S4.SS1.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathit{chem}(a,\!b,\{c\})=\frac{|0.08-0.01|}{0.05}=1.4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>𝑐ℎ𝑒𝑚</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mn>0.08</mn><mo>−</mo><mn>0.01</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mn>0.05</mn></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mn>1.4</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathit{chem}(a,\!b,\{c\})=\frac{|0.08-0.01|}{0.05}=1.4</annotation></semantics></math>.
As <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{c\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{c\}</annotation></semantics></math> is the only relevant subset, the LLM chemistry for (<math id="S4.SS1.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>a</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a</annotation></semantics></math>,
<math id="S4.SS1.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>b</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b</annotation></semantics></math>) is also <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1.4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1.4</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1.4</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">ChemE</span> yields the following main result:</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmtheorem1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmtheorem1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 1</span></span><span id="Thmtheorem1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmtheorem1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmtheorem1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmtheorem1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">LLM Chemistry emerges in <math id="Thmtheorem1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math> as a function of the MIG iff models
exhibit heterogeneous performance <math id="Thmtheorem1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(q_{i},a_{i})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(q_{i},a_{i})</annotation></semantics></math> profiles; for (near-)identically performing models, cost-based selection
pressure vanishes, so no interaction effects can be detected (chemistry = <math id="Thmtheorem1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0</annotation></semantics></math>).
</span>(Proof in Appendix <a href="#A1.SS2" title="A.2 Theoretical Analysis of Chemistry Emergence ‣ Appendix A Appendix ‣ LLM Chemistry Estimation for Multi-LLM Recommendation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A.2</span></a>)<span id="Thmtheorem1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">This novel, counter-intuitive finding suggests that model diversity
(i.e., model performance diversity) is essential for chemistry estimation:
near-maximal performance makes chemistry-based multi-LLM recommendation
unnecessary, while near-zero performance makes them infeasible.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS1.p5.1" class="ltx_p">A direct consequence of Theorem <a href="#Thmtheorem1" title="Theorem 1. ‣ 4.1 Computing LLM Chemistry ‣ 4 Computing LLM Chemistry for Multi-LLM Recommendation ‣ LLM Chemistry Estimation for Multi-LLM Recommendation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> is that chemistry
scales with model performance diversity, as formalized in Corollary <a href="#Thmcorollary1" title="Corollary 1. ‣ 4.1 Computing LLM Chemistry ‣ 4 Computing LLM Chemistry for Multi-LLM Recommendation ‣ LLM Chemistry Estimation for Multi-LLM Recommendation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>:</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmcorollary1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_corollary">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmcorollary1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Corollary 1</span></span><span id="Thmcorollary1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmcorollary1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmcorollary1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmcorollary1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Chemistry in <math id="Thmcorollary1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math> varies monotonically with model performance diversity:
as diversity increases, chemistry either always increases or always
decreases, depending on task type.
</span>(Proof in Appendix <a href="#A1.SS2" title="A.2 Theoretical Analysis of Chemistry Emergence ‣ Appendix A Appendix ‣ LLM Chemistry Estimation for Multi-LLM Recommendation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A.2</span></a>)<span id="Thmcorollary1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text"></span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.p6" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS1.p6.1" class="ltx_p">Theorem <a href="#Thmtheorem1" title="Theorem 1. ‣ 4.1 Computing LLM Chemistry ‣ 4 Computing LLM Chemistry for Multi-LLM Recommendation ‣ LLM Chemistry Estimation for Multi-LLM Recommendation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> and Corollary <a href="#Thmcorollary1" title="Corollary 1. ‣ 4.1 Computing LLM Chemistry ‣ 4 Computing LLM Chemistry for Multi-LLM Recommendation ‣ LLM Chemistry Estimation for Multi-LLM Recommendation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> establish the theoretical
basis for LLM Chemistry, which we now operationalize in the next subsection to
select effective ensembles for a given query, directly addressing the central
question posed in the introduction.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.2 </span>Multi-LLM Recommendation</h3>

<figure id="S4.F2" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2510.03930v1/llm-chem.png" id="S4.F2.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:357/102;" width="357" height="102" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 2: </span>Illustration of LLM Chemistry estimation process for multi-LLM recommendation. A snapshot of the performance histories is provided in Appendix <a href="#A1.SS5" title="A.5 Benchmark Sample Runs ‣ Appendix A Appendix ‣ LLM Chemistry Estimation for Multi-LLM Recommendation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A.5</span></a>.</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S4.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We illustrate our LLM Chemistry estimation process for multi-LLM recommendation
process in Figure <a href="#S4.F2" title="Figure 2 ‣ 4.2 Multi-LLM Recommendation ‣ 4 Computing LLM Chemistry for Multi-LLM Recommendation ‣ LLM Chemistry Estimation for Multi-LLM Recommendation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>. The figure outlines the main stages of a multi-LLM recommendation session, from an initial MIG construction for <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q</annotation></semantics></math> from past performance histories to LLM Chemistry estimation, to the recommendation of an optimal LLM configuration.
The central goal of this process
is to recommend a set of LLMs that maximizes the
benefit of
collaboration while minimizing the cost of answering <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q</annotation></semantics></math>.
To formalize this,
we define
<math id="S4.SS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{X}=\{X_{1},X_{2},\ldots,X_{n}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{X}=\{X_{1},X_{2},\ldots,X_{n}\}</annotation></semantics></math> as a collection of non-empty subsets
of <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math>, where each <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{i}\subseteq S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>⊆</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{i}\subseteq S</annotation></semantics></math>
is a candidate LLM configuration.
<span id="S4.SS2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Subsets may overlap, meaning the
same LLM can appear in multiple configurations</span>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">To evaluate how well these subsets
group related LLMs, we define a loss function <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}(\mathcal{X})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}(\mathcal{X})</annotation></semantics></math>
that measures unrealized chemistry potential for <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{X}</annotation></semantics></math>.
<math id="S4.SS2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}(\mathcal{X})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}(\mathcal{X})</annotation></semantics></math> has two components:
(1)
inter-subset loss <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{inter}(\mathcal{X})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{inter}(\mathcal{X})</annotation></semantics></math>,
the potential lost between LLMs placed into different subsets,
and (2) intra-subset loss <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{intra}(\mathcal{X})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{intra}(\mathcal{X})</annotation></semantics></math>,
the potential not realized within a subset compared to the theoretical maximum.
Two parameters control these trade-offs: <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha\in[0,1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha\in[0,1]</annotation></semantics></math> balances the components, and <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math> penalizes larger subsets to favor smaller, targeted recommendations (Equation <a href="#S4.E3" title="In 4.2 Multi-LLM Recommendation ‣ 4 Computing LLM Chemistry for Multi-LLM Recommendation ‣ LLM Chemistry Estimation for Multi-LLM Recommendation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>).</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<table id="S4.E3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}(\mathcal{X})=\alpha\cdot\mathcal{L}_{inter}(\mathcal{X})+(1-\alpha)\cdot\mathcal{L}_{intra}(\mathcal{X})+\beta\cdot|\mathcal{X}|" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}(\mathcal{X})=\alpha\cdot\mathcal{L}_{inter}(\mathcal{X})+(1-\alpha)\cdot\mathcal{L}_{intra}(\mathcal{X})+\beta\cdot|\mathcal{X}|</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(3)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<table id="S4.EGx1" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S4.E4"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathcal{L}_{inter}(\mathcal{X})=\sum_{\begin{subarray}{c}a,b\in S\\
a\neq b,\text{ different subsets}\end{subarray}}\mathit{chem}(a,\!b,S)-\sum_{\begin{subarray}{c}X_{i},X_{j}\in\mathcal{X}\\
i&lt;j\end{subarray}}\sum_{a\in X_{i}}\sum_{\begin{subarray}{c}b\in X_{j}\\
b\neq a\end{subarray}}\mathit{chem}(a,\!b,S)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mtable rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><mrow><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mrow><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>≠</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mtext> different subsets</mtext></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable></munder></mstyle><mrow><mi>𝑐ℎ𝑒𝑚</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>S</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mtable rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi></mrow></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable></munder></mstyle><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></munder></mstyle><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mtable rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>≠</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable></munder></mstyle><mrow><mi>𝑐ℎ𝑒𝑚</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>S</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathcal{L}_{inter}(\mathcal{X})=\sum_{\begin{subarray}{c}a,b\in S\\
a\neq b,\text{ different subsets}\end{subarray}}\mathit{chem}(a,\!b,S)-\sum_{\begin{subarray}{c}X_{i},X_{j}\in\mathcal{X}\\
i&lt;j\end{subarray}}\sum_{a\in X_{i}}\sum_{\begin{subarray}{c}b\in X_{j}\\
b\neq a\end{subarray}}\mathit{chem}(a,\!b,S)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(4)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<table id="S4.EGx2" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S4.E5"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathcal{L}_{intra}(\mathcal{X})=\sum_{a&lt;b\in S}\mathit{chem}(a,\!b,S)-\sum_{X_{i}\in\mathcal{X}}\sum_{a&lt;b\in X_{i}}\mathit{chem}(a,\!b,S)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>b</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></munder></mstyle><mrow><mi>𝑐ℎ𝑒𝑚</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>S</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi></mrow></munder></mstyle><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>b</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></munder></mstyle><mrow><mi>𝑐ℎ𝑒𝑚</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>S</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathcal{L}_{intra}(\mathcal{X})=\sum_{a&lt;b\in S}\mathit{chem}(a,\!b,S)-\sum_{X_{i}\in\mathcal{X}}\sum_{a&lt;b\in X_{i}}\mathit{chem}(a,\!b,S)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(5)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS2.p4.1" class="ltx_p">These loss terms capture unrealized chemistry from separation (inter-subset) and incomplete groupings (intra-subset<span id="footnote2" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">2</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">2</sup>
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">2</span>
              
              
              
            In Equation <a href="#S4.E5" title="In 4.2 Multi-LLM Recommendation ‣ 4 Computing LLM Chemistry for Multi-LLM Recommendation ‣ LLM Chemistry Estimation for Multi-LLM Recommendation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>, <math id="footnote2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a&lt;b\in S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>b</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a&lt;b\in S</annotation></semantics></math> denotes an arbitrary ordering, with each unordered pair <math id="footnote2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{a,b}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{a,b}</annotation></semantics></math> counted once.</span></span></span>) of LLMs in <math id="S4.SS2.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{X}</annotation></semantics></math>. In this paper, we set <math id="S4.SS2.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha=0.5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0.5</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha=0.5</annotation></semantics></math> to weight them equally and <math id="S4.SS2.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta=0.5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0.5</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta=0.5</annotation></semantics></math> to moderately penalize large subsets, typically recommending up to <math id="S4.SS2.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="10" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>10</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">10</annotation></semantics></math> LLMs.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS2.p5.1" class="ltx_p">Algorithm <a href="#algorithm2" title="In 4.2 Multi-LLM Recommendation ‣ 4 Computing LLM Chemistry for Multi-LLM Recommendation ‣ LLM Chemistry Estimation for Multi-LLM Recommendation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a> recommends the optimal LLM subset from historically-derived
configurations by leveraging pre-computation and the error function in
Equation <a href="#S4.E3" title="In 4.2 Multi-LLM Recommendation ‣ 4 Computing LLM Chemistry for Multi-LLM Recommendation ‣ LLM Chemistry Estimation for Multi-LLM Recommendation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>. Rather than exploring the exponential space of all possible
subsets (<math id="S4.SS2.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2^{|S|}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>S</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2^{|S|}</annotation></semantics></math>), it evaluates only <math id="S4.SS2.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> historical subsets as starting points
and performs local optimization through hill climbing with at most <math id="S4.SS2.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="iters" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">iters</annotation></semantics></math>
iterations per starting point.
This algorithm has a runtime complexity of <math id="S4.SS2.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="O(n\times iters\times|S|^{3})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>S</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mn>3</mn></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">O(n\times iters\times|S|^{3})</annotation></semantics></math>, which is polynomial in the input size and
exponentially better than the brute-force approach of evaluating all subsets.
This algorithm systematically explores the search space by starting from each
historical subset in <math id="S4.SS2.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{X}</annotation></semantics></math>, and applying local search through single LLM
additions, removals, or swaps to find a better configuration. It uses the
<span id="S4.SS2.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">loss</span> function to evaluate each candidate solution and guide the
optimization process.
Specifically, <span id="S4.SS2.p5.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">loss</span> computes intra-subset
chemistry as <math id="S4.SS2.p5.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sum_{a&lt;b\in X}\mathit{chem}(a,b,S)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>b</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow></msub><mrow><mi>𝑐ℎ𝑒𝑚</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>S</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sum_{a&lt;b\in X}\mathit{chem}(a,b,S)</annotation></semantics></math> and inter-subset
chemistry as <math id="S4.SS2.p5.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sum_{a\in X}\sum_{b\in S\setminus X}\mathit{chem}(a,b,S)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow></msub><mrow><msub><mo lspace="0.167em">∑</mo><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>∖</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mrow><mi>𝑐ℎ𝑒𝑚</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>S</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sum_{a\in X}\sum_{b\in S\setminus X}\mathit{chem}(a,b,S)</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<figure id="algorithm2" class="ltx_figure ltx_algorithm ltx_minipage ltx_align_middle ltx_align_floatright ltx_framed ltx_framed_top" style="width:186.8pt;">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="algorithm2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Algorithm 2</span> </span>Optimal LLM Subset Recommendation for <math id="algorithm2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q</annotation></semantics></math>.</figcaption>
<div id="algorithm2.4" class="ltx_listing ltx_lst_numbers_left ltx_framed ltx_framed_topbottom ltx_listing">
<div id="algorithm2.4.1" class="ltx_listingline">



<span id="algorithm2.4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Function :</span> <span id="algorithm2.4.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_markedasmath ltx_font_smallcaps">Recommend</span>
</div>
<div id="algorithm2.4.2" class="ltx_listingline"><span id="algorithm2.4.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Input :</span> LLMs <math id="algorithm2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math>, query <math id="algorithm2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q</annotation></semantics></math>, factor <math id="algorithm2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math>, max iters.
</div>
<div id="algorithm2.4.3" class="ltx_listingline"><span id="algorithm2.4.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Output :</span> Optimal subset <math id="algorithm2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>X</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X^{*}</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="algorithm2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q</annotation></semantics></math>.
</div>
<div id="algorithm2.4.4" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="algorithm2.4.4.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">1</span></span>
            <math id="algorithm2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="best\leftarrow\emptyset" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∅</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">best\leftarrow\emptyset</annotation></semantics></math>; <math id="algorithm2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="minLoss\leftarrow\infty" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>L</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">minLoss\leftarrow\infty</annotation></semantics></math>
</div>
<div id="algorithm2.4.5" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="algorithm2.4.5.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">2</span></span>
            Get <math id="algorithm2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{X}_{Q}=\{X_{1},\ldots,X_{n}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mi>Q</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{X}_{Q}=\{X_{1},\ldots,X_{n}\}</annotation></semantics></math> from past runs
</div>
<div id="algorithm2.4.6" class="ltx_listingline"><span id="algorithm2.4.6.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">// </span><span id="algorithm2.4.6.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">Precompute using <span id="algorithm2.4.6.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">ChemE</span> </span>
</div>
<div id="algorithm2.4.7" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="algorithm2.4.7.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">3</span></span>
