<p id="S5.T2.3" class="ltx_p ltx_figure_panel ltx_minipage ltx_align_center ltx_align_middle" style="width:397.5pt;"><span id="S5.T2.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Note:</span> <math id="S5.T2.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>r</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r</annotation></semantics></math> = Pearson’s correlation coefficient. Results shown for
strongest significant relationships per task type. Significance (Sig.) levels: <math id="S5.T2.m15" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="***" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo rspace="0em">∗</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∗</mo><mo lspace="0em">∗</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">***</annotation></semantics></math>
<math id="S5.T2.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p&lt;0.001" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>0.001</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p&lt;0.001</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S5.T2.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="**" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>∗</mo><mo lspace="0em">⁣</mo><mo>∗</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">**</annotation></semantics></math> <math id="S5.T2.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p&lt;0.01" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>0.01</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p&lt;0.01</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S5.T2.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="*" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>∗</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">*</annotation></semantics></math> <math id="S5.T2.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p&lt;0.05" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>0.05</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p&lt;0.05</annotation></semantics></math>. <span id="S5.T2.3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_underline ltx_font_italic">Key finding:</span>
LLM-Chemistry-ensemble complementarity relationship reverses by task type (<math id="S5.T2.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r=-0.154" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>0.154</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r=-0.154</annotation></semantics></math> vs <math id="S5.T2.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r=0.319" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0.319</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r=0.319</annotation></semantics></math>).</p></div></div>
</figure>
<div id="S5.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">We treat task complexity as an orthogonal lens to the task-type categories to examine whether chemistry effects scale with difficulty (Table <a href="#S5.T3" title="Table 3 ‣ 5.2 Results and Analysis ‣ 5 Experiments ‣ LLM Chemistry Estimation for Multi-LLM Recommendation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>).
For statement credibility classification (low complexity),
chemistry scores correlate positively with complementarity as group size
increases (<math id="S5.SS2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r=0.118" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0.118</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r=0.118</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S5.SS2.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p&lt;0.05" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>0.05</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p&lt;0.05</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="S5.SS2.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N=5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N=5</annotation></semantics></math>; <math id="S5.SS2.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r=0.319" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0.319</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r=0.319</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S5.SS2.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p&lt;0.001" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>0.001</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p&lt;0.001</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="S5.SS2.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N=10" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>10</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N=10</annotation></semantics></math>),
while effectiveness shows no significant association. For
clinical note summarization (medium complexity), chemistry shows no association
in smaller groups but correlates positively with complementarity at <math id="S5.SS2.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N=10" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>10</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N=10</annotation></semantics></math>
(<math id="S5.SS2.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r=0.148" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0.148</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r=0.148</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S5.SS2.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p&lt;0.01" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>0.01</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p&lt;0.01</annotation></semantics></math>). For automated program
repair (high complexity), chemistry scores correlate negatively with
complementarity in medium groups (<math id="S5.SS2.p3.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r=-0.154" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>0.154</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r=-0.154</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S5.SS2.p3.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p&lt;0.01" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>0.01</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p&lt;0.01</annotation></semantics></math>) but
positively with effectiveness across all group sizes (<math id="S5.SS2.p3.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N=3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N=3</annotation></semantics></math>: <math id="S5.SS2.p3.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r=0.189" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0.189</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r=0.189</annotation></semantics></math>,
<math id="S5.SS2.p3.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p&lt;0.001" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>0.001</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p&lt;0.001</annotation></semantics></math>; <math id="S5.SS2.p3.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N=5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N=5</annotation></semantics></math>: <math id="S5.SS2.p3.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r=0.144" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0.144</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r=0.144</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S5.SS2.p3.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p&lt;0.05" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>0.05</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p&lt;0.05</annotation></semantics></math>; <math id="S5.SS2.p3.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N=10" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>10</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N=10</annotation></semantics></math>: <math id="S5.SS2.p3.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r=0.229" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0.229</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r=0.229</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S5.SS2.p3.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p&lt;0.001" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>0.001</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p&lt;0.001</annotation></semantics></math>). These results indicate that task complexity moderates
chemistry’s role: in low-complexity tasks, chemistry enhances complementarity
without affecting effectiveness; in
medium-complexity tasks, it begins to matter only for larger ensembles; and in high-complexity tasks, chemistry reduces
complementarity but consistently improves effectiveness.</p>
</div>
